@{
    ViewData["Title"] = "How Recomp Works";
    ViewData["OnboardingPage"] = true;
}

<div class="onboarding-page">
    <section class="onboarding-hero">
        <div class="marketing-kicker">WELCOME TO 12 WEEK RECOMP</div>
        <h1>Train. Refill. Lean Out. Repeat.</h1>
        <p class="onboarding-lede">
            Recomp coordinates training, carbohydrate, protein, recovery, and energy balance so hard
            work is fueled while fat loss happens in a controlled way.
        </p>
        <div class="onboarding-motivation">It’s Easy When You Find What Motivates You!</div>
    </section>

    <section class="onboarding-section">
        <div class="onboarding-section-head">
            <span>1</span>
            <div>
                <div class="marketing-kicker">THE FUEL CYCLE</div>
                <h2>Glycogen is stored carbohydrate your muscles can use quickly.</h2>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="fuel-cycle-grid">
            <article class="fuel-cycle-card burn">
                <div class="fuel-cycle-icon">01</div>
                <h3>TRAIN → USE IT</h3>
                <p>Hard or high-volume training draws down muscle glycogen. The amount depends on the workout, duration, intensity, and muscles used.</p>
            </article>
            <article class="fuel-cycle-card refill">
                <div class="fuel-cycle-icon">02</div>
                <h3>CARBS → REFILL IT</h3>
                <p>Carbohydrate after training restores glycogen. After substantial depletion, adequate carbohydrate can restore a large share of stores within about a day.</p>
            </article>
            <article class="fuel-cycle-card protect">
                <div class="fuel-cycle-icon">03</div>
                <h3>PROTEIN → REPAIR</h3>
                <p>Protein supplies amino acids for repair and growth. Recomp keeps protein high while using carbohydrate strategically for the work that actually needs it.</p>
            </article>
        </div>

        <div class="onboarding-explainer">
            <strong>What about “burning protein for glucose”?</strong>
            <p>
                The real process is <b>gluconeogenesis</b>. Your liver can make glucose from lactate,
                glycerol, and some amino acids when carbohydrate availability is low. That is normal
                physiology—not an automatic muscle-loss switch. Recomp avoids unnecessarily deep
                depletion around demanding training so amino acids can stay focused on recovery and
                muscle remodeling while protein intake remains high.
            </p>
        </div>
    </section>

    <section class="onboarding-section">
        <div class="onboarding-section-head">
            <span>2</span>
            <div>
                <div class="marketing-kicker">THE RECOMP OPPORTUNITY</div>
                <h2>Fuel the hard work. Use a modest deficit when demand is lower.</h2>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="recomp-window">
            <div class="recomp-window-step">
                <span>HARD TRAINING</span>
                <strong>Higher glycogen demand</strong>
            </div>
            <div class="recomp-window-arrow">→</div>
            <div class="recomp-window-step highlight">
                <span>REFEED / RECOVER</span>
                <strong>Carbs + protein restore readiness</strong>
            </div>
            <div class="recomp-window-arrow">→</div>
            <div class="recomp-window-step">
                <span>LOWER-DEMAND DAYS</span>
                <strong>Controlled deficit supports fat loss</strong>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="onboarding-note-grid">
            <div>
                <h3>No starvation switch</h3>
                <p>
                    The brain does not flip a simple “fat-storage mode” switch when glycogen falls.
                    But sustained low energy availability can produce metabolic and hormonal adaptations.
                    Recomp therefore avoids turning every day into a severe deficit.
                </p>
            </div>
            <div>
                <h3>No fixed 72-hour window</h3>
                <p>
                    Glycogen status is rolling. How long you stay well-fueled depends on how depleted
                    you were, how much carbohydrate you eat, and what training comes next. Recomp
                    estimates that balance day by day instead of assuming a fixed two- or three-day rule.
                </p>
            </div>
            <div>
                <h3>Flexible food, not a cheat day</h3>
                <p>
                    During higher-carb recovery periods, foods can be flexible as long as you remain
                    inside the day’s calorie and macro targets. Whole foods are still the default for
                    health, fullness, and micronutrients.
                </p>
            </div>
        </div>
    </section>

    <section class="onboarding-section app-process-section">
        <div class="onboarding-section-head">
            <span>3</span>
            <div>
                <div class="marketing-kicker">WHAT THE APP DOES</div>
                <h2>You provide a few real-world inputs. Recomp keeps recalculating the plan.</h2>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="app-process-grid">
            <article>
                <div class="app-process-number">1</div>
                <h3>Plan your week</h3>
                <p>Enter the workouts you intend to do. Change them any time. Recomp estimates the expected glycogen and recovery demand.</p>
            </article>
            <article>
                <div class="app-process-number">2</div>
                <h3>Track what you eat</h3>
                <p>Use any convenient food-tracking app—even a free one—and enter your daily calories, protein, carbs, and fat into Recomp.</p>
            </article>
            <article>
                <div class="app-process-number">3</div>
                <h3>Log the workout you actually did</h3>
                <p>Planned and actual are kept separate. If the session changed, the fuel and recovery math can change too.</p>
            </article>
            <article>
                <div class="app-process-number">4</div>
                <h3>Check in and weigh in</h3>
                <p>Weight is useful; a smart scale that estimates body fat and lean mass adds context. Those estimates can fluctuate with hydration, so Recomp looks at trends.</p>
            </article>
        </div>

        <div class="app-output-panel">
            <div>
                <div class="marketing-kicker">THEN RECOMP ADAPTS</div>
                <h3>Your targets move with what actually happened.</h3>
            </div>
            <ul>
                <li>Estimates rolling glycogen demand and restoration.</li>
                <li>Sets daily calorie, protein, carbohydrate, and fat targets.</li>
                <li>Adjusts for yesterday’s intake and today’s planned workload.</li>
                <li>Can suggest pre-workout carbohydrate when fuel is short.</li>
                <li>Can recommend modifying training when pain, illness, or recovery data says the plan should change.</li>
            </ul>
        </div>
    </section>

    <section class="onboarding-start">
        <div>
            <div class="marketing-kicker">READY?</div>
            <h2>Give Recomp your baseline and planned week.</h2>
            <p>You do not need perfect data. Start with what you know; the system gets more useful as actual data accumulates.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="onboarding-start-actions">
            <a class="marketing-cta" asp-controller="Intake" asp-action="Index">Start My Setup →</a>
            <a class="marketing-text-link" asp-controller="Home" asp-action="Evidence">Read the science</a>
        </div>
    </section>

    <section class="onboarding-sources">
        <strong>Science behind this explanation:</strong>
